{"assets":["/editorial-assets/c0bc09cdbf455cb5d34498b9c51a313b47257a291d3f07c98cd276546bc0fa75.png","/editorial-assets/f9edda04d142988747c93c4e7ffb9141d5687b7dd90520c29948f28573b3d1a1.png","/editorial-assets/ac75352c3aa4c356e61bc03c82fbe52974cf80ddb95f163faf37b86068b9b4a1.png","/editorial-assets/17ca3e0823e2775d19068494e9a383eb8e0aee7d7e80f96f9ce8cbb3de60591a.png","/editorial-assets/255b7554be3186680196df73e2cf484bd974b55b9e72f1d19659208157f37075.png","/editorial-assets/456b861988b889b001fe48542633f27e37132efb02feca9d5efb0ab17a274211.png"],"contestId":"893","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"problemCode":"893A"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/893/problem/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"893A - Chess For Three"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"This task is about pure implementation. Maintain the number of current spectator and check if he doesn't win. With knowledge of current winner "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"w"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and current spectator "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" you can easily get the third player by formula 6 - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"w"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" (just the sum of all numbers without the known ones)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Overall complexity: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"893B"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/893/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"893B - Beautiful Divisors"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's notice that there are only 8 beautiful numbers less than 10"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"5"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Generate them all and select the greatest one which is also divisor of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Overall complexity: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(1)."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"893C"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/893/problem/C"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"893C - Rumor"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In this problem you are given an undirected graph with weighted vertices. And the problem is to calculate the sum of minimum values in every connected component. To do this we just need to run DFS or BFS several times."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"893D"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/893/problem/D"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"893D - Credit Card"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The following greedy solution works. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Firstly, deposite money only on days with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 0. Secondly, every time the balance is negative to the day with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 0, refill it to maximal possible value such that it won't go over "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" later. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":true},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" Days with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≠ 0 can only lead to invalid state by going over card limit. We can only add money to the balance. Adding zero money in those days won't make invalid states if all were valid previously. Finally, if it's possible to get valid state in every day then it's possible to get it by refilling the same day the check happens. For example, you can make "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"max"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(0, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"currentBalance"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") balance in those days. Then you will never have negative balance there. Though it's not the most optimal way. "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" Let "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"delta"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" be some value you deposite in some day with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 0 to pass all conditions till the next day with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 0. I state that function of number of game's moves dependancy on "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"delta"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is monotonious. Let's check it for some fixed "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Define minimum balance you will get on any suffix from now as "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"minBal"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Obviously, taking "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1 will make it "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"minBal"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1. If it goes negative then you will need an extra move to finish the game. Thus taking maximal "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"delta"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" will lead to the lowest score possible. "}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"And last but not least — realization part. What will be the maximum possible value to deposite? Actually, it's such a value that optimal game after this will lead to maximum balance of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in some day. Thus, you want to check what will be the maximum balance "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"maxBal"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" if you add zero money and take "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"delta"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" as "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"maxBal"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Obviously, if it's negative then output  - 1. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Naively this still works on "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") per day and lead to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") overall. Notice that by depositing "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"delta"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" you increase maximums on each suffix for now by "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"delta"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". So, you can calculate it as you will do nothing and add sum "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"deltaSum"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" of your "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"delta"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"'s to get actual value. You store prefix sum of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" up to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"pr"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Then take maximum on suffix for every "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"su"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" is the maxumum "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"pr"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"delta"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"deltaSum"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"su"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"I hope I made it clear enough. :D"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Overall comlpexity: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"893E"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/893/problem/E"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"893E - Counting Arrays"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Fill the array with ones. Now we should take every prime divisor "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and distribute "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"cnt"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" (maximum power of this prime to appear in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") of it into some cells of the array. It is pretty well-known problem, it's equal to "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/c0bc09cdbf455cb5d34498b9c51a313b47257a291d3f07c98cd276546bc0fa75.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". Take product of this values for every prime "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". This will be the answer if there were no negative numbers. But we should also multiply it by number of ways to select even number of position to put unary minuses — 2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" (like you can fill in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1 position anyhow and the final one will be determined by parity of current count)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"To process many queries you should factorize numbers in "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/f9edda04d142988747c93c4e7ffb9141d5687b7dd90520c29948f28573b3d1a1.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" (by precalcing the smallest prime divisor of every number up to 10"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"6"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" with sieve of Eratosthenes), get "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/ac75352c3aa4c356e61bc03c82fbe52974cf80ddb95f163faf37b86068b9b4a1.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(1) (by precalcing factorials and inverse factorials) and get 2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" in "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/f9edda04d142988747c93c4e7ffb9141d5687b7dd90520c29948f28573b3d1a1.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" (binary exponentiation)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Overall complexity: "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/17ca3e0823e2775d19068494e9a383eb8e0aee7d7e80f96f9ce8cbb3de60591a.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"893F"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/893/problem/F"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"893F - Subtree Minimum Query"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The main idea is to use a two-dimensional data structure: one dimension is depth of vertices, and other dimension is the time we entered a vertex during DFS."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Model solution uses sparse table for these purposes. First of all, let's renumerate the vertices so we can handle them easier. We run DFS from the root and then sort the vertices by their depth (and if depths are equal, by time we entered them in DFS). Then we renumerate vertices in this sorted order."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We need to denote some functions in order to continue:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":false},"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"D"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") — depth of vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in the tree; "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"tin"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") — the time we entered "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" during DFS; "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"tout"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") — the time we left "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" during DFS. "}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For each depth we can store a sorted array of vertices belonging do this depth. This will allow us to build an auxiliary sparse table "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"lf"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"lf"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] is such vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" that:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":false},"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"D"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"D"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") + 2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"; "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"tin"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"tin"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"); "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"tin"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") is minimal among all vertices that meet first two conditions. "}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We also need a second sparse table "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"rg"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"rg"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" iff:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":false},"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"D"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"D"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") + 2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"; "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"tout"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"tout"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"); "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"tout"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") is minimal among all vertices that meet first two conditions. "}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"These sparse tables can be built using binary search in arrays we created for depths."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Okay, why do we need them? To create a third sparse table that will process the queries themselves:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"table"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] — the minimum value of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" among vertices "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" such that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" belongs to 2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"-blocked subtree of some vertex with index included in ["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"). This table can be built backwards with the help of auxiliary tables."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"So, how do we answer the queries? We need to look at the binary representation of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1 and do something like binary lifting (but descending the tree instead of ascending) and maintain the leftmost and the rightmost vertices on current depth which belong to the subtree we are interested in (and make queries to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"table"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" on the segment between these two vertices)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"This solution works in "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/255b7554be3186680196df73e2cf484bd974b55b9e72f1d19659208157f37075.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", but, unfortunately (or fortunately to some participants), we made the time limit too high so the structures that require "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/456b861988b889b001fe48542633f27e37132efb02feca9d5efb0ab17a274211.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" time to process each query, such as two-dimensional segment trees, might also get AC."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"}],"kind":"document"},"schema":2,"sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/55989"}